19:["$","h2",null,{"children":"BMI scale and healthy body weight"}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"children":"The usual adult BMI scale identifies 18.5 to 24.9 as the healthy weight range. Our calculator translates that range into a practical weight interval for your height. That interval is not an “ideal” body shape and it is not a target to pursue at all costs. Healthy body weight looks different across life stages, cultures, body frames, and medical histories. For many adults, improving blood pressure, stamina, sleep, or food habits matters more than reaching a particular number on the scale."}]
1b:["$","h2",null,{"children":"BMI for women and BMI for men"}]
1c:["$","p",null,{"children":["Standard adult BMI categories are the same for women and men. Nonetheless, people with the same BMI can have different proportions of fat and lean tissue. Hormonal changes, pregnancy, menopause, and life stage can shape weight distribution for women. Men may carry more weight around the abdomen. These differences are reasons to broaden the health picture, not reasons to treat the calculation as irrelevant. Visit our ",["$","$L5",null,{"href":"/bmi-women","className":"text-teal-700 underline","children":"BMI for women guide"}]," or ",["$","$L5",null,{"href":"/bmi-men","className":"text-teal-700 underline","children":"BMI for men guide"}]," for a fuller discussion."]}]
1d:["$","h2",null,{"children":"BMI by age: adults, seniors, and children"}]
1e:["$","p",null,{"children":"For adults, the categories do not change with age. In later life, however, unintentional weight loss, reduced muscle mass, falls, chronic illness, and frailty make the story more nuanced. Older adults benefit from individualized advice that protects strength, protein intake, mobility, and independence. BMI for children is different again. Children and teenagers are assessed using BMI-for-age growth charts and percentiles, not adult cutoffs. A paediatric clinician can interpret those charts in the context of growth and development."}]
1f:["$","h2",null,{"children":"When BMI is less informative"}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":"Athletes and people with substantial muscle mass can have a higher BMI without having excess body fat. Pregnancy, fluid retention, limb loss, and some medical conditions can also affect the meaning of the number. Ethnicity can matter as well: some populations experience metabolic risks at lower BMI levels. The best next step may be a clinician’s assessment, waist measurement, body-composition test, or simply a discussion of symptoms and lifestyle."}]
21:["$","h2",null,{"children":"BMI versus body fat percentage"}]
22:["$","p",null,{"children":"BMI uses only height and weight. Body fat percentage estimates how much of body weight is fat tissue. That extra detail can be useful, but measurement methods vary in accuracy. Home scales using bioelectrical impedance can change with hydration. Skinfold measurements and DEXA scans require technique or equipment. For most people, a consistent trend in multiple measures is more useful than a single supposedly perfect number."}]
23:["$","h2",null,{"children":"A healthy weight plan built around habits"}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":"If you would like to gain, lose, or maintain weight, begin with habits you can repeat. Eat regular meals with vegetables or fruit, fibre-rich carbohydrates, protein, and fats that fit your culture and budget. Make water your default drink. Plan movement you enjoy: brisk walking, cycling, swimming, dancing, or strength training all count. Adults generally benefit from at least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity, plus muscle-strengthening work on two or more days, if appropriate for their health."}]
25:["$","p",null,{"children":"Rapid, restrictive plans often backfire. A safer approach is gradual change, adequate sleep, and support from a registered dietitian or clinician when needed. Do not use a BMI result to skip meals, overexercise, or start a supplement. If eating, weight, or body image feels distressing, compassionate professional support is available."}]
26:["$","h2",null,{"children":"Nutrition, exercise, water, and sleep"}]
27:["$","p",null,{"children":"There is no single meal plan for every body. A useful plate includes a source of protein, plants, and a satisfying carbohydrate. Strength exercise preserves muscle during weight loss and supports function during aging. Water needs vary with weather, food, activity, pregnancy, and health conditions; drink regularly and seek clinical advice if you have fluid restrictions. Sleep is often overlooked: a steady sleep schedule can support appetite regulation, energy, and recovery."}]
28:["$","h2",null,{"children":"How to use this healthy weight calculator"}]
29:["$","p",null,{"children":"Enter your age, sex, height, and weight. Choose metric or imperial units. The BMI calculator immediately displays your BMI value, category, and an estimated healthy-weight range for your height. Use the result as a point of orientation. If you have a chronic condition, are pregnant, are under 18, have had recent unexplained weight change, or are considering major changes to food or exercise, discuss the result with a qualified professional."}]
